WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.029
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:10.839
Our endeavors in creating amazingly detailed
fluid simulations is often hamstrung by the

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:15.719
fact that we need to simulate the motion of
tens of millions of particles. Needless to

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.730
say this means excruciatingly long computation
times and large memory consumption.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.789
This piece of work tries to alleviate the
problem by confining the usage of particles

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:31.779
to a narrow band close to the liquid surface
and thus, decimating the number of particles

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:37.700
used in the simulation. The rest of the simulation
is computed on a very coarse grid, where we

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.469
compute quantities of the fluid like velocity
and pressure in gridpoints, and instead of

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.850
computing them everywhere, we try to guess
what is happening between these gridpoints.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:54.219
The drawback of this is that we may miss a
lot of details because of that. And the brilliant

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.530
part of this new technique is that we only
use a cheap, sparse grid where there is not

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.930
a lot of things happening, and use the expensive
particles only near the surface, where there

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.950
are a lot of details we can capture well.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:13.770
The FLIP term that you see in the video means
Fluid Implicit Particle, a popular way of

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.650
simulating fluids that uses both grids and
particles.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:23.710
In this scene, the old method uses 24 million
particles, while the new technique uses only

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:29.640
one million, and creates closely matching
results. You can see a lot of excess particles

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.130
in the footage with the classical simulation
technique, and the foamish-looking version

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.650
is the proposed new, more efficient algorithm.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.280
Creating such a technique is anything but
trivial. Unless special measures are taken,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.630
the simulation may have robustness issues,
which means that there are situations where

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.840
it does not produce a sensible result.
This is demonstrated in a few examples where

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.170
with the naive version of the technique, a
piece of fluid never ever comes to rest, or

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.700
it may exhibit behaviors that are clearly
unstable.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.780
It also takes approximately half as much time
to run the simulation, and uses half as much

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.920
memory, which is such a huge relief for visual 
effects artists.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:14.920
I don't know about you Fellow Scholars, but
I see a flood of amazing fluid papers coming

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.980
in the near future and I'm having quite a
bit of trouble containing my excitement. Exciting

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.670
times are ahead indeed.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.890
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

